Report: Base16 length calculations and formatting-error conversion
3 claims; 0 removal(s).
  run_ids: ["b246e0e7-9411-476d-ba79-1e005da23076"] -> ["e4272827-123d-4edd-9140-034dd29063a5"]
  explanation: "For base16ct 1.0.0, decoded_len terminates normally for every input slice. It returns Ok of exactly half the input length when that length is even, and Err(InvalidLength) exactly when it is odd. It examines length only, so this result does not validate hexadecimal characters. encoded_len returns exactly twice the input length without a panic when the length is at most usize::MAX / 2. The handwritten From<Error> conversion into core::fmt::Error always returns the formatting error value normally for either source error, intentionally discarding which error occurred. These are body proofs of the unchanged published APIs; the length proofs quantify arbitrary slice lengths representable in the model. Separately, original Error Display formatting under default {} flags and a fixed initialized64-byte writer returns exactly invalid Base16 encoding (23bytes) or invalid Base16 length (21bytes) for the corresponding variant. A writer that rejects the first literal before storing bytes instead propagates fmt::Error and retains its original length and bytes. Both variants and writer modes are symbolic; one Kani harness completes1910checks with no failures." -> "For base16ct 1.0.0, decoded_len terminates normally for every input slice. It returns Ok of exactly half the input length when that length is even, and Err(InvalidLength) exactly when it is odd. It examines length only, so this result does not validate hexadecimal characters. encoded_len returns exactly twice the input length without a panic when the length is at most usize::MAX / 2. The handwritten From<Error> conversion into core::fmt::Error always returns the formatting error value normally for either source error, intentionally discarding which error occurred. These are body proofs of the unchanged published APIs; the length proofs quantify arbitrary slice lengths representable in the model. Separately, original Error Display formatting under default {} flags and a fixed initialized64-byte writer returns exactly invalid Base16 encoding (23bytes) or invalid Base16 length (21bytes) for the corresponding variant. A writer that rejects the first literal before storing bytes instead propagates fmt::Error and retains its original length and bytes. Both variants and writer modes are symbolic; one Kani harness completes1910checks with no failures. Additional original-code Kani checks cover lower/upper/mixed decode for every2-byte input and every initial3-byte destination: success occurs exactly for the accepted alphabet, returns exactly1decoded byte, and preserves destination bytes1and2; invalid input returns InvalidEncoding while byte0 is not specified. Lower/upper encode checks cover every1-byte input and initial4-byte destination, return its exact canonical2ASCII bytes, preserve bytes2and3 and roundtrip through the actual decoder."
